-- three workers behind one loop
status: workers 3 active 0 accepted 0 completed 0 lost 0 replaced 0 draining 0
-- a TCP client
t> echo hello, worker
t< hello, worker
t> upper kern passes descriptors
t< KERN PASSES DESCRIPTORS
t> sum 1 2 3 40
t< 46
t> sum 1 two
t< ERR sum takes whole numbers
t> sum 18446744073709551615 1
t< ERR sum overflows
t> whoami
t< ERR whoami needs a Unix-domain connection
t> frobnicate
t< ERR unknown command
t> quit
t< bye
then the worker closes the connection: yes
-- a Unix-domain client
whoami is the user and group the client sees at its own peer: yes
u> quit
u< bye
-- four connections, each to the worker with the fewest
a> worker
a< worker 0 incarnation 1
b> worker
b< worker 1 incarnation 1
c> worker
c< worker 2 incarnation 1
d> worker
d< worker 0 incarnation 1
-- b quits, and the next connection goes where it was
b> quit
b< bye
e> worker
e< worker 1 incarnation 1
-- e sets an idle limit and says nothing more
e> idle 25
e< idle limit 25 ms
e< idle: closing
then the worker closes the connection: yes
-- d makes its worker crash; a is served by the same worker
d's connection is closed: yes
a's connection is closed: yes
status: workers 3 active 1 accepted 7 completed 4 lost 2 replaced 1 draining 0
f> worker
f< worker 0 incarnation 2
-- Terminate: nothing new is accepted, what is open is served
a new connection is refused: yes
c> echo still served
c< still served
c> quit
c< bye
f> sum 20 22
f< 42
f> quit
f< bye
the server's log:
  3 workers started
  worker 0 died with 2 connections in flight; replaced
  draining: 2 connections in flight
  every worker drained before the deadline
-- a second server: one worker, and a client that stays past the deadline
g> echo here to stay
g< here to stay
the connection is closed when the deadline has passed: yes
the server's log:
  1 worker started
  draining: 1 connection in flight
  worker 0 was stopped at the deadline with 1 connection in flight
-- afterwards
as many descriptors are open as before the servers: yes
